  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 336 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nMid-level water vapor imagery shows a large swath of moisture \nstreaming into the central Plains all the way from Baja California. \nThis is mainly in response to the deepening trough over the desert \nsouthwest where heavy rain has lead to many flooding events in the \npast 24 hours. Over the next few days, the shortwave trough across \nthe southwest will slowly ride over the synoptic ridge that remains \nparked over the ArkLaTex region. Continuous PVA into central Kansas \nand then into eastern Kansas will give way to enough lift to support \nwidespread rain (and some weak thunderstorms) chances into Saturday \nmorning. Through today, majority of the synoptic lift will remain \nconcentrated across western and central Kansas. This should keep \nbest rain chances there for much of the day today. With ample cloud \ncover and precipitation chances, temperatures will not warm much, \nmainly staying in the low to mid 70s. Upper level lift shifts east \nlate tonight into Friday morning as the western trough pushes the \nupper ridge over the southern US towards the Gulf. Scattered chances \nfor showers will continue across the area for much of Friday morning \nand early afternoon until later in the evening when the main \nshortwave trough axis pushes northeast across Kansas. Isentropic \nascent across eastern Kansas will increase in response to the \ndeepening surface trough with widespread showers expected Friday \nevening into early morning Saturday. With PWATs Friday evening \nranging between 1.5-2\", heavy rainfall, especially areas that see \ntraining of heavy rain showers and thunderstorms, could lead to \nlocalized flooding across eastern Kansas. \n\nRain chances decrease west to east through the day Saturday as mid-\nlevel subsidence begins to move in from the west. This should usher \nin a mostly dry period Sunday and Monday with temperatures \nincreasing slightly, topping out in the upper 70s and low 80s. Long \nrange guidance has decent agreement in a stout longwave trough \ndigging south out of the PNW by Tuesday and Wednesday that will \nusher in our next chance of precipitation and cooler temperatures. \nGuidance is still in a bit of disagreement with how it evolves and \npropagates the upper system across the central US for late next week \nso temperature spreads remain high and PoPs remain low confidence.",
